WEBVTT

NOTE
The film carries its own burnt-in captions, so this track describes what is on
screen rather than repeating the overlay word for word. It is offered, not
switched on by default: a viewer who can see the picture already has the text.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
[music begins] The title card. "Reprise — one Rust core. Four native
frontends."

2
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.500
Reprise on GNOME, the whole application, with the seek bar drawn from the
audio rather than left an empty gutter.
"One player. Everything you listen to."

3
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:12.000
Subscribed podcasts, their episodes and the position each one was left at.
"Podcasts — shows, episodes, where you stopped"

4
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.500
Subscribed YouTube channels, their new videos listed like any other source.
"YouTube channels — new videos, played as audio"

5
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:21.000
The releases page, filled from the artists in the library.
"New releases — from the artists you keep"

6
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.500
Upcoming concerts, matched to the same artists.
"Concerts nearby — for the same artists"

7
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:30.000
The listening statistics page.
"Your listening, counted"

8
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.800
The device page: a phone connected over MTP, the playlists chosen for it and
the transfer profile. Then the frame moves for the only time in the film and
the desktop hands over to the handset.
"Sync to your phone — playlists over MTP, transcoded on the way"

9
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:45.000
The same library on Android, browsed and played from the phone.
"Reprise on Android — the same library, in your hand"

10
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:54.600
The visualizer on the phone — the same engine as on the desktop, rendered by
Android rather than rewritten for it, moving with the track that is playing.
"The same visuals — in time with the music"

11
00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:58.200
The end card. The mark lands with the same moves it made on the title card.
